1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
In the previous lessons, you built agents that present rich UIs,

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.400
but only inside of the chat window.

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.198
In this lesson, you'll go beyond the chat window

4
00:00:08.198 --> 00:00:09.999
to build an app where the agent

5
00:00:09.999 --> 00:00:11.800
and the frontend share live state.

6
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.288
We will build an agentic take

7
00:00:14.288 --> 00:00:16.388
on the classic to-do app,

8
00:00:16.487 --> 00:00:19.088
where the agent creates to-dos from the backend,

9
00:00:19.487 --> 00:00:22.088
users edit or complete those in the front end,

10
00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:24.588
and both sides stay in sync automatically.

11
00:00:24.987 --> 00:00:29.487
This state synchronization is the foundation for building your own Claude code

12
00:00:29.812 --> 00:00:32.612
or Cursor style application across different verticals.

13
00:00:32.811 --> 00:00:35.612
from legal to accounting to marketing to, you name it.

14
00:00:36.311 --> 00:00:38.468
So, let's get to it. We are

15
00:00:38.468 --> 00:00:40.625
now venturing beyond the isolated chat interaction

16
00:00:40.625 --> 00:00:42.825
to explore fully featured full-stack agents,

17
00:00:42.825 --> 00:00:44.825
which are designed to feel like true co-workers.

18
00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:47.825
It turns out that a small set of agent user interaction primitives

19
00:00:47.825 --> 00:00:50.625
are used by virtually all successful full-stack agents.

20
00:00:50.924 --> 00:00:53.125
Before we explore a couple of those technical primitives,

21
00:00:53.125 --> 00:00:55.458
it's worth taking a step back and briefly asking, what

22
00:00:55.458 --> 00:00:58.024
makes for a great Fullstack Agentic App from a product standpoint?

23
00:00:58.325 --> 00:01:00.725
Of course, a great intuitive user experience is the foundation.

24
00:01:00.924 --> 00:01:02.908
As we mentioned before, there is no reason

25
00:01:02.908 --> 00:01:04.459
to run away from the chat modality

26
00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:06.009
which has taken the world by storm.

27
00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:09.509
And remember that chat also does not mean text only interaction.

28
00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:12.808
You want to build augmented chat, which leverages

29
00:01:12.808 --> 00:01:15.709
Generative UI, voice interactions, and perhaps even video.

30
00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:18.009
A great chat experience also gives users

31
00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:20.108
visibility into what the agent is doing.

32
00:01:20.209 --> 00:01:22.909
To keep them engaged, but more importantly, to earn user's trust

33
00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:25.009
and to give users the opportunity to

34
00:01:25.009 --> 00:01:27.409
steer the agent to better address their needs.

35
00:01:27.992 --> 00:01:30.192
So this is a good place to experiment. Just as critically,

36
00:01:30.192 --> 00:01:32.492
chat as the anchor of a agentic interaction

37
00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.692
does not mean that chat exists in isolation.

38
00:01:34.992 --> 00:01:38.293
The agent should feel like a native part of a larger application.

39
00:01:38.793 --> 00:01:40.893
It should be able to leverage any data, context,

40
00:01:40.992 --> 00:01:43.793
connectors, or actions associated with the application at large.

41
00:01:44.192 --> 00:01:45.406
A good rule of thumb is that

42
00:01:45.406 --> 00:01:46.793
if an end user can take some action

43
00:01:46.793 --> 00:01:48.992
or learn some fact using the application at large,

44
00:01:49.293 --> 00:01:51.185
they should also be able to take that same

45
00:01:51.185 --> 00:01:53.076
action or learn the same fact via the chat.

46
00:01:53.076 --> 00:01:56.876
The agent must also have deep access to the application's real-time context,

47
00:01:56.977 --> 00:01:59.277
meaning to what the user is currently looking at

48
00:01:59.376 --> 00:02:01.876
and to the actions currently available to the user.

49
00:02:02.076 --> 00:02:04.277
We can tease out these qualities with a few examples.

50
00:02:04.576 --> 00:02:06.876
In Cursor and in GitHub Copilot,

51
00:02:06.977 --> 00:02:08.377
the agent isn't a chat window you

52
00:02:08.377 --> 00:02:09.977
switch to, it's integrated into the workspace itself.

53
00:02:10.276 --> 00:02:13.477
It reads your code, understands your project, edits files, runs commands,

54
00:02:13.676 --> 00:02:15.276
all without you leaving the editor.

55
00:02:15.576 --> 00:02:17.877
The agent and the tool are a single entity.

56
00:02:18.161 --> 00:02:19.928
In Notion AI, the agent is

57
00:02:19.928 --> 00:02:21.991
automatically aware of what's on the page.

58
00:02:21.991 --> 00:02:24.741
You don't need to paste context or explain what you're working on.

59
00:02:24.741 --> 00:02:27.360
It already knows because it's embedded in the document,

60
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.907
not sitting beside it. In Harvey, a legal AI assistant,

61
00:02:30.907 --> 00:02:33.108
the agent doesn't just answer questions,

62
00:02:33.108 --> 00:02:35.407
it takes actions alongside the user,

63
00:02:35.407 --> 00:02:37.807
drafting documents, analyzing contracts,

64
00:02:37.807 --> 00:02:40.407
operating inside the same workflow the lawyer is using.

65
00:02:40.407 --> 00:02:42.407
Again, it's a collaborator, not a chatbot.

66
00:02:42.707 --> 00:02:45.907
It turns out that agentic applications across very different domains

67
00:02:45.907 --> 00:02:48.707
end up being built atop the same small set of core primitives.

68
00:02:48.707 --> 00:02:50.590
One of those building blocks is the Generative

69
00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:52.707
UI spectrum that we explored in the previous lessons.

70
00:02:53.207 --> 00:02:56.207
In this lesson, we'll look at two additional technical building blocks.

71
00:02:56.286 --> 00:02:58.885
Frontend Tool calling and Shared State synchronization.

72
00:02:58.885 --> 00:03:00.686
We will explore them from two perspectives.

73
00:03:00.686 --> 00:03:02.485
First, from the standpoint of developers,

74
00:03:02.485 --> 00:03:06.186
the cases you should know about in day-to-day development. And second, conceptually,

75
00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:09.086
so you can understand how these paradigms work under the hood.

76
00:03:09.352 --> 00:03:11.451
First, frontend tool calls.

77
00:03:11.451 --> 00:03:14.352
Frontend tool calls are directly analogous to the standard backend tool calls

78
00:03:14.352 --> 00:03:16.852
you're likely already familiar with.

79
00:03:16.852 --> 00:03:19.352
The difference is that they allow an agent to execute an action

80
00:03:19.352 --> 00:03:21.852
directly in the frontend application, not just where it's running.

81
00:03:21.852 --> 00:03:25.352
In React, you register frontend tools using the useFrontendTool hook,

82
00:03:25.352 --> 00:03:28.352
specifying the arguments expected by the tool

83
00:03:28.352 --> 00:03:30.852
and the handler to be executed when the tool is called.

84
00:03:30.852 --> 00:03:32.852
If your handler returns a result,

85
00:03:32.852 --> 00:03:34.852
that result is passed back to the agent

86
00:03:34.852 --> 00:03:37.252
as a standard tool call result. You can register

87
00:03:37.286 --> 00:03:39.286
frontend tool calls in a centralized fashion

88
00:03:39.286 --> 00:03:41.786
when your application initializes, or you can disperse their definitions

89
00:03:41.786 --> 00:03:44.086
across different parts of the app, so that they load and unload

90
00:03:44.086 --> 00:03:47.186
automatically as users traverse the application.

91
00:03:47.385 --> 00:03:49.135
Under the hood, these tool calls are passed

92
00:03:49.135 --> 00:03:50.885
to the agentic backend in a standard format.

93
00:03:51.086 --> 00:03:53.786
When the agent then decides to execute a frontend tool,

94
00:03:53.786 --> 00:03:55.485
the AG-UI recognizes that,

95
00:03:55.485 --> 00:03:57.286
pauses the backend execution,

96
00:03:57.286 --> 00:03:59.086
hands off control to the frontend,

97
00:03:59.086 --> 00:04:02.186
executes that tool, and then transfers control

98
00:04:02.186 --> 00:04:04.786
back to the agentic backend once execution is complete.

99
00:04:05.219 --> 00:04:06.835
Once again, you don't have to

100
00:04:06.835 --> 00:04:08.719
worry about all this complexity when developing,

101
00:04:08.819 --> 00:04:11.719
you simply call the useFrontendTool hook.

102
00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.620
Next, shared state synchronization.

103
00:04:14.719 --> 00:04:17.720
By default, the agent and the front end are disconnected.

104
00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:19.720
The agent does not know what the user sees,

105
00:04:19.819 --> 00:04:22.019
and the front end does not know what the agent knows.

106
00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:24.915
Shared state is the primitive that solves this.

107
00:04:25.314 --> 00:04:29.415
To use shared state synchronization, use your agent's standard state abstractions.

108
00:04:29.714 --> 00:04:32.235
In this lesson, we will use LangChain's agent state.

109
00:04:32.634 --> 00:04:34.845
On the frontend, use the useAgent hook,

110
00:04:35.144 --> 00:04:37.925
which exposes a reactively updated, typed state.

111
00:04:38.197 --> 00:04:40.396
object. Under the hood, here's how this works.

112
00:04:40.406 --> 00:04:42.497
As the agent run, it can update its state.

113
00:04:42.507 --> 00:04:45.096
When it does so, it can then update the frontend state

114
00:04:45.107 --> 00:04:47.096
by emitting state delta events,

115
00:04:47.107 --> 00:04:49.096
meaning it communicates the changes

116
00:04:49.107 --> 00:04:51.697
that needs to be applied to the frontend state

117
00:04:51.706 --> 00:04:54.048
so that it remains in sync with the backend state.

118
00:04:54.048 --> 00:04:56.848
These state delta events are themselves streamed

119
00:04:56.848 --> 00:04:59.149
because they're often generated by an LLM.

120
00:04:59.149 --> 00:05:02.048
The front end can also emit state delta events

121
00:05:02.048 --> 00:05:04.949
to support the state modifications that are coming from the user.

122
00:05:04.949 --> 00:05:07.949
Finally, when needed, conflict resolution between the two states

123
00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:10.548
is handled via AG-UI middleware.

124
00:05:10.867 --> 00:05:13.067
Once again, these are implementation details

125
00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:15.367
you do not need to be aware of in day-to-day development.

126
00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:17.367
Simply use the useAgent hook.

127
00:05:17.666 --> 00:05:20.666
In this lesson, you will build a canvas agentic to-do app

128
00:05:20.666 --> 00:05:23.867
that combines a chat interface with a traditional to-do board.

129
00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:26.666
You will expose a open to-do canvas tool to the agent

130
00:05:26.666 --> 00:05:28.374
to allow the agent to interact

131
00:05:28.374 --> 00:05:30.367
with the application on the user's behalf.

132
00:05:30.666 --> 00:05:33.367
And you will use state synchronization to keep the front-end

133
00:05:33.367 --> 00:05:36.367
representation of to-dos synced bidirectionally

134
00:05:36.367 --> 00:05:39.963
with the agent's representation of to-dos. Let's look at the code.

135
00:05:39.963 --> 00:05:42.764
We're going to be building an agentic to-do app

136
00:05:43.363 --> 00:05:45.564
that will venture outside of the chat window

137
00:05:45.564 --> 00:05:47.963
to a fully featured full-stack agentic application.

138
00:05:48.163 --> 00:05:51.564
Just like before, we'll start with our reset lesson cell.

139
00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:53.670
to make sure we're on a clean state.

140
00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:56.470
We'll set up our project dependencies.

141
00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.470
Once again, starting with our Python dependencies,

142
00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.470
and followed by our frontend dependencies.

143
00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:11.670
Before we move on, we'll also load up our API keys

144
00:06:11.769 --> 00:06:13.870
that are provided by the DeepLearning platform.

145
00:06:17.553 --> 00:06:19.754
We're now ready to load back

146
00:06:20.254 --> 00:06:21.853
our familiar starting point

147
00:06:22.153 --> 00:06:24.454
by starting the frontend and the agent server.

148
00:06:24.675 --> 00:06:27.975
We'll once again use a standard LangChain deep agent

149
00:06:28.274 --> 00:06:30.475
and serve it behind a FastAPI server.

150
00:06:30.875 --> 00:06:32.675
Now we're ready to start our frontend.

151
00:06:34.675 --> 00:06:37.175
Once again, we have our basic chat setup

152
00:06:37.175 --> 00:06:39.475
and ready for modification and augmentation.

153
00:06:39.675 --> 00:06:44.175
We're going to amplify this basic chat with frontend tool calling

154
00:06:44.175 --> 00:06:47.475
as well as state synchronization between the frontend and the agent.

155
00:06:47.675 --> 00:06:50.875
We'll begin by implementing shared state between the agent and the frontend.

156
00:06:50.923 --> 00:06:55.023
First, we're going to define a schema for our agent state.

157
00:06:55.023 --> 00:06:58.923
Our agent state consists of an array of to-dos, where each to-do

158
00:06:58.923 --> 00:07:01.423
has an ID, a title, and a flag

159
00:07:01.423 --> 00:07:04.023
indicating whether the to-do has been completed or not.

160
00:07:04.023 --> 00:07:06.023
We've defined our agent state.

161
00:07:06.023 --> 00:07:08.411
We're now going to define tools that will allow our

162
00:07:08.411 --> 00:07:10.911
agent to interact with its own state.

163
00:07:11.211 --> 00:07:14.211
The first tool we define is the manage_todos tool.

164
00:07:14.511 --> 00:07:17.411
This tool lets the agent update its own state

165
00:07:17.411 --> 00:07:20.211
using the Command function provided by LangChain.

166
00:07:20.511 --> 00:07:22.911
The second tool we provide is the get_todos tool,

167
00:07:22.911 --> 00:07:25.511
which allows the agent to read its own state.

168
00:07:25.899 --> 00:07:29.100
Finally, we put both of these tools in an array

169
00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:32.699
so that we can easily populate them into our agent later.

170
00:07:33.399 --> 00:07:34.784
All right. Now that we have

171
00:07:34.784 --> 00:07:36.399
the agent state and the tools defined,

172
00:07:36.399 --> 00:07:39.100
we're going to be updating our agent to take advantage of those.

173
00:07:39.408 --> 00:07:42.108
This code replaces the previous agent we defined

174
00:07:42.208 --> 00:07:44.708
with a new LangChain deep agent.

175
00:07:44.807 --> 00:07:47.108
This LangChain deep agent is still very basic

176
00:07:47.208 --> 00:07:51.007
with a few key changes. First, it specifies a state schema.

177
00:07:51.117 --> 00:07:53.916
using the AgentState type we created earlier.

178
00:07:54.117 --> 00:07:56.317
Second, it specifies the todo_tools

179
00:07:56.317 --> 00:07:58.416
that are now made available to the agent.

180
00:07:58.617 --> 00:08:01.916
Lastly, just like before, we're specifying CopilotKitMiddleware

181
00:08:01.916 --> 00:08:04.292
to allow the CopilotKit and the UI stacks

182
00:08:04.292 --> 00:08:06.817
to easily interact with the LangChain agent.

183
00:08:06.824 --> 00:08:08.824
Now, that we set up of the agent, it's time to set up the

184
00:08:08.824 --> 00:08:10.824
frontend to take advantage of this new functionality.

185
00:08:10.875 --> 00:08:13.625
Now we're going to introduce frontend tool calls to our agent,

186
00:08:13.675 --> 00:08:15.798
as well as bind the agent state

187
00:08:15.798 --> 00:08:18.225
we just defined to our frontend state bidirectionally.

188
00:08:18.274 --> 00:08:22.524
Once again, because we're editing TypeScript code inside of this Jupyter notebook,

189
00:08:22.531 --> 00:08:25.331
we must fully overwrite the file that was defined before.

190
00:08:25.432 --> 00:08:27.732
But let's walk through the changes we made.

191
00:08:27.831 --> 00:08:30.331
First, we're going to define a simple local variable

192
00:08:30.432 --> 00:08:32.631
that our application is going to take advantage of.

193
00:08:32.673 --> 00:08:35.874
todosOpen and setTodosOpen initially sets to false.

194
00:08:36.274 --> 00:08:38.438
We're then going to define a frontend tool

195
00:08:38.438 --> 00:08:40.874
that's going to be made available to the agent

196
00:08:40.874 --> 00:08:43.274
so that the agent can interact with this local variable.

197
00:08:43.673 --> 00:08:46.573
We are using the useFrontendTool hook provided by CopilotKit,

198
00:08:46.874 --> 00:08:49.374
which connects to AG-UI's frontend tool concept.

199
00:08:49.974 --> 00:08:51.747
And just like every other tool, this

200
00:08:51.747 --> 00:08:53.774
frontend tool can provide a name, a description,

201
00:08:54.274 --> 00:08:57.974
parameters, specified once again as a Zod schema.

202
00:08:58.577 --> 00:09:02.508
As a reminder, Zod is the TypeScript equivalent of Pydantic.

203
00:09:02.807 --> 00:09:05.177
And finally, our frontend tool can have a handler

204
00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:06.942
that will be executed when the agent

205
00:09:06.942 --> 00:09:08.957
decides to call this tool on the frontend.

206
00:09:09.187 --> 00:09:11.687
Notice that the frontend tool handler can be asynchronous.

207
00:09:11.888 --> 00:09:14.988
Under the hood, whenever an agent decides to call a frontend tool,

208
00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:17.687
the AG-UI middleware hands off execution from

209
00:09:17.687 --> 00:09:20.187
the agentic backend to the agentic frontend.

210
00:09:20.388 --> 00:09:22.488
Once a result is provided by the frontend,

211
00:09:22.488 --> 00:09:24.587
it is propagated back to the agentic backend.

212
00:09:24.633 --> 00:09:26.732
and the agentic loop continues.

213
00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:29.633
To recap what we did here, we created a local variable

214
00:09:29.633 --> 00:09:32.033
that allows the application to open a to-dos panel.

215
00:09:32.033 --> 00:09:34.332
Initially this to-do's panel is set to false.

216
00:09:34.332 --> 00:09:36.732
And we give the agent the ability to interact

217
00:09:36.732 --> 00:09:39.133
with this variable via a dedicated frontend tool call.

218
00:09:39.332 --> 00:09:40.948
Now we're going to subscribe to

219
00:09:40.948 --> 00:09:42.832
our agent state using the useAgent hook.

220
00:09:43.153 --> 00:09:45.952
useAgent is an incredibly powerful primitive

221
00:09:46.352 --> 00:09:48.352
for the agentic age on the frontend.

222
00:09:48.452 --> 00:09:50.352
It gives you everything you need on the frontend

223
00:09:50.452 --> 00:09:53.452
in order to interact with agents with all of the messy details

224
00:09:53.552 --> 00:09:55.253
automatically taken care of for you.

225
00:09:55.346 --> 00:09:59.046
In this case, we're going to be using useAgent's state variable

226
00:09:59.346 --> 00:10:03.046
in order to bidirectionally tie our frontend state to the agent state.

227
00:10:03.346 --> 00:10:06.846
Finally, we're returning the React component associated with this application now.

228
00:10:07.145 --> 00:10:09.546
The most important part I would like to point out here

229
00:10:09.645 --> 00:10:12.846
is the TodoList component, which accepts a state getter

230
00:10:12.946 --> 00:10:15.346
and a state setter for its own to-dos.

231
00:10:15.654 --> 00:10:18.154
The state getter is simply reading

232
00:10:18.154 --> 00:10:20.154
agent.state.todos.

233
00:10:20.455 --> 00:10:22.455
The state setter is simply setting

234
00:10:22.455 --> 00:10:24.455
agent.state with new todos.

235
00:10:24.754 --> 00:10:26.315
This is quite profound.

236
00:10:26.315 --> 00:10:30.125
We see here that the useAgent hook provides us with full-featured

237
00:10:30.125 --> 00:10:32.725
state synchronization between our front end and our back end.

238
00:10:32.945 --> 00:10:36.225
Just like local standard React state, the state is reactive,

239
00:10:36.445 --> 00:10:38.485
which we can take advantage of in our application.

240
00:10:38.559 --> 00:10:41.549
And every act of state update streaming

241
00:10:41.549 --> 00:10:44.389
and context resolution is automatically handled for us.

242
00:10:44.789 --> 00:10:48.460
As application developers, we can simply treat it as standard state

243
00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:51.240
as if it was just defined in our local application.

244
00:10:51.759 --> 00:10:54.440
Under the hood, it's automatically kept in sync with our agentic backend.

245
00:10:54.784 --> 00:10:56.784
All right, let's give it a try.

246
00:10:56.784 --> 00:10:58.784
Once again, we're going to use the display_app helper

247
00:10:58.784 --> 00:11:00.585
to display our application in line.

248
00:11:00.784 --> 00:11:02.784
All right, you see here we have now a chat

